In both PostgreSQL and MS SQL Server, the `TABLESAMPLE` clause can only appear in a SELECT statement of a table. However in the SQL:2003 standard, the TABLESAMPLE clause can [appear almost anywhere a table source was expected](https://wiki.postgresql.org/wiki/TABLESAMPLE_Implementation):

```sql
DELETE FROM foo TABLESAMPLE SYSTEM (1);               -- ಠ_ಠ
UPDATE foo TABLESAMPLE SYSTEM (1) SET col1 = 'col1';  -- ಠ_ಠ
```

I suggest we don't support these syntax as they make no sense, and I can't find any DBMS implementing it. 

<details><summary>The clause syntax for variant dialects:</summary>

```ebnf
(* SQL standard since 2003 *)
sample_clause = "TABLESAMPLE" ( "BERNOULLI" | "SYSTEM" ) '(' number ')' 
                [ "REPEATABLE" '(' number ')' ];

(* PostgreSQL 9+ *)
(* https://www.postgresql.org/docs/13/sql-select.html *)
sample_clause = "TABLESAMPLE" identifier '(' argument [',' ...] ')' 
                [ "REPEATABLE" '(' number ')' ];

(* MS SQL *)
(* https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/from-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver15 *)
sample_clause = "TABLESAMPLE" [ "SYSTEM" ] '(' number [ "PERCENT" | "ROWS" ] ')' 
                [ "REPEATABLE" '(' number ')' ];

(* Oracle *)
(* https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/20/sqlrf/SELECT.html#GUID-CFA006CA-6FF1-4972-821E-6996142A51C6 *)
sample_clause = "SAMPLE" [ "BLOCK" ] '(' number ')' 
                [ "SEED" '(' number ')' ];

(* Snowflake *)
(* https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/constructs/sample.html *)
sample_clause = ( "TABLESAMPLE" | "SAMPLE" ) [ "BERNOULLI" | "ROW" | "SAMPLE" | "BLOCK" ] '(' number [ "ROWS" ] ')'
                [ ( "REPEATABLE" | "SEED" ) '(' number ')' ];
```

</details>

MySQL does not support TABLESAMPLE yet, but [work has been done in the backend](https://dev.mysql.com/worklog/task/?id=9127) to support sampling. 

----

Assuming we don't require Oracle compatibility, for best compatibility, our syntax would look like a mix of PostgreSQL and SQL Server:

```ebnf
sample_clause = "TABLESAMPLE" [ "BERNOULLI" | "SYSTEM" ] '(' number [ "PERCENT" | "ROWS" ] ')'
                [ "REPEATABLE" '(' number ')' ]
```

now the question is how do the sampling method we actually want fit in:

```sql
-- this doesn't make sense at all.
SELECT * FROM tbl TABLESAMPLE REGIONS (33.3 PERCENT);

-- may work, but not much better than using LIMIT directly.
-- and need to consider what to do when the input number of rows exceeds the number of regions
-- though the number is an approximation anyway.
SELECT * FROM tbl TABLESAMPLE REGIONS (5 ROWS);

-- PostgreSQL's syntax actually requires at least one argument, so this isn't really a compatibility solution
-- plus for this we need to consider meaning of TABLESAMPLE BERNOULLI ();
SELECT * FROM tbl TABLESAMPLE REGIONS ();

-- ...?
SELECT * FROM tbl TABLESAMPLE REGIONS (NULL);
SELECT * FROM tbl TABLESAMPLE REGIONS (MAXVALUE);

-- create a special case for REGIONS, not treating it as a sampling_method
SELECT * FROM tbl TABLESAMPLE REGIONS;
```

----

we should ensure applying a TABLESAMPLE to a view results in an error.

```sql
CREATE VIEW v AS ...;

SELECT * FROM v TABLESAMPLE ...;
-- on PostgreSQL: 0A000 TABLESAMPLE clause can only be applied to tables and materialized views

```

